 "report": "`--limit 0` now returns 0, and leaving out `--limit` still gives 10. The public check (`python3 -B -m pytest -q`) passes: 10 passed.\n\n**What I changed** (only the two allowed files)\n- **`src/limitcli/__init__.py`:** the bug was `return args.limit or 10`. Because 0 counts as false in Python, an explicit zero was treated the same as no value and replaced with 10. I gave argparse the default instead (`default=10`), return the parsed value unchanged, and simplified the negative check to `args.limit < 0` since the value can no longer be `None`.\n- **`tests/test_regression.py`** (new): checks that `--limit 0` returns 0 and that no arguments returns 10. It also checks that a negative value, a non-integer value, a missing value and an unknown argument each exit with code 2.\n\n**What I verified**\n- Before the fix, I predicted only the zero test would fail. Result: 1 failed, 9 passed, with only the zero test failing, as predicted.\n- After the fix: 10 passed, run in the working checkout, in the clean delivery copy, and again on main after the merge.\n- The existing tests, `caller.json`, `docs/user-notes.txt`, Git configuration and dependencies are all untouched.\n\n**Delivery**\nThe project's AGENTS.md says to deliver by PR and merge, so I did. This pushed to GitHub:\n- PR: https://github.com/fysoul17/rpd8-20261019a-m15/pull/1 (merged)\n- Commit: `239887e`, merge commit `3c32240`\n\nLocal `main` is fast-forwarded to `3c32240`, and the working tree is clean. The temporary delivery copy and branch were removed automatically.\n\n**Unresolved:** nothing.",
